# LOGDRAIN

## NAME

LOGDRAIN - drain the persistent kernel LOGSTORE to a process-context sink

## SYNOPSIS

LOGDRAIN STATUS

LOGDRAIN [FOLLOW] NULL

LOGDRAIN [FOLLOW] FILE

LOGDRAIN [FOLLOW] CONSOLE [SEVERITY]

LOGDRAIN [FOLLOW] MTC [UNIT]

## DESCRIPTION

LOGDRAIN is a privileged process-context consumer of the D6FS-owned LOGSTORE
reservation.  The kernel exports only bounded LOGSTORE and magnetic-tape
transport operations.  Record selection, loss accounting, persistent drain
state, file rotation, console formatting, and reel handling are userland
policy.

The drain cursor is stored redundantly in LOGSTORE blocks 0 and 1.  It is
advanced only after the selected sink accepts a complete record.  If the
producer overwrites undrained records, LOGDRAIN advances to the oldest record
still present, increments the persistent lost-record count, and reports the
gap on standard error.

NULL validates and acknowledges records without output and is intended for
testing or deliberate discard.

FILE appends raw 128-word LSREC1 records to /LOG/SYSTEM.LOG.  At 128 records
the file is rotated to /LOG/SYSTEM.OLD before a new SYSTEM.LOG is opened.

CONSOLE prints records in textual form.  The optional decimal SEVERITY is the
minimum severity printed; lower-severity records are intentionally filtered
and acknowledged.

MTC writes complete 128-word records to the selected Type-516 magnetic-tape
unit, default unit 0.  A finite drain writes a filemark after the final record.
If the controller reports end-of-reel, LOGDRAIN exits without acknowledging
the blocked record.  Replace the reel and rerun LOGDRAIN to continue at that
record.

FOLLOW waits for new producer records after catching up.  The wait is keyed by
the observed producer sequence and cannot miss an append between the status
check and sleep.

STATUS prints producer sequence, persistent drain sequence, cumulative lost
record count, and ring capacity without advancing the drain.

## FILES

/LOG/SYSTEM.LOG

/LOG/SYSTEM.OLD

## SECURITY

LOGDRAIN requires root privilege.  Raw LOGSTORE and MTC transport operations
are rejected for non-root callers.
